<p>
    <em>Antichamber</em> is the most difficult game in the <a href="/epiphanygames/">Epiphany Games list</a>
    for me to rate, because it is the first one I played.
    I played it ten years ago, three years before I played the second-longest-ago-played game in the list, Subnautica.
    Back then, I thought it was the coolest game I had ever played, and I tried to get my friends to play it
    (although not as zealously as I now try to get my friends to play <a href="/epiphanygames/outerwilds/">Outer Wilds</a>).
</p>
The world design in Antichamber is very simplistic, with a lot of featureless hallways.
And yet, it manages to have a more mysterious atmosphere than similarly simple first-person puzzle box games such as Lingo and Chroma Zero.
I think part of it is that it actually has sound.
It also pulls off the simple graphics much better
<p>
    I replayed it recently before writing this review. Is it still a cool game?
    Yeah, but it's not as mind-blowing as it was, and it only took me 2 hours to beat again.
</p>
Generic wise words, which you have to link to the specifics of the puzzle
<p>
    <em>Antichamber</em> pioneered a non-Portal version of "non-Euclidean geometry",
    which is now seen in other games such as <a href="/epiphanygames/lingo/">Lingo</a>.
    It is used very tastefully, and not overdone.
</p>
Doesn't lean on mazeness, which is good because that got tedious in Lingo
<p>
    The puzzles in Antichamber feature one of two themes:
    Physics not working the way you would expect it to,
    or manipulation of blocks of matter.
</p>
<p>
    The block-of-matter puzzles can be circumvented by progressing further.
</p>
decimeter-sized blocks
<p>
    Antichamber is a short game, so it doesn't make sense to waste a bunch of bytes on this review.
    Just check it out and see if you like it as much as I did.
</p>

    {%- call sidenote("sn-noneuclidean") %}
    Upon doing research for this article, I have learned that the term
    "non-Euclidean geometry" traditionally refers to either hyperbolic geometry or elliptical geometry.
    Antichamber still features standard parallel lines, so it uses normal Euclidean geometry, but with some wormholes.
    It's more like an impossible object.
    {% endcall -%}.

